@@ -293,7 +293,7 @@
           "source_section": "Definition",
           "quote": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring and let $A$ be a commutative $R$-algebra with\nstructure map $\\eta_A\\colon R\\to A$ ([[def-algebra-over-a-commutative-ring]]).\n\n**The subalgebra generated by finitely many elements.** Let $n\\in\\mathbb N$ and\n$a_1,\\ldots,a_n\\in A$. Iterating the universal property of a polynomial ring\n([[thm-universal-property-of-a-polynomial-ring]]) along the recursion\n$R[x_1,\\ldots,x_{k+1}]=R[x_1,\\ldots,x_k][x_{k+1}]$ of\n[[def-multivariate-polynomial-ring-by-iteration]] gives a unique unital ring\nhomomorphism\n\n$$\\operatorname{ev}\\colon R[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]\\longrightarrow A$$\n\nthat agrees with $\\eta_A$ on constants and sends $x_i$ to $a_i$ for each $i$;\nat each step of the recursion the one-variable universal property supplies\nexistence and uniqueness of the extension, and at $n=0$ the map is $\\eta_A$\nitself. Its image is written $R[a_1,\\ldots,a_n]$ and is called the\n**$R$-subalgebra of $A$ generated by $a_1,\\ldots,a_n$**. It is a subring of $A$\ncontaining $\\eta_A(R)$, and it is the smallest such subring containing\n$a_1,\\ldots,a_n$, since any subring with those properties is closed under the\nsums and products that make up a polynomial expression. At $n=0$ it is\n$\\eta_A(R)$, the image of $R$ in $A$.\n\n**Finite type.** $A$ is **of finite type over $R$**, equivalently a **finitely\ngenerated $R$-algebra**, when $A=R[a_1,\\ldots,a_n]$ for some $n\\in\\mathbb N$ and\nsome $a_1,\\ldots,a_n\\in A$. Equivalently, $A$ is isomorphic as an $R$-algebra to\na quotient $R[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]/\\mathfrak a$ for some $n\\in\\mathbb N$ and some\nideal $\\mathfrak a$: the map $\\operatorname{ev}$ above is then surjective, and\n[[thm-first-isomorphism-theorem-rings]] identifies $A$ with\n$R[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]/\\ker\\operatorname{ev}$; conversely the composite of the\ncanonical projection with the inclusion of the indeterminates exhibits any such\nquotient as generated by the residues of $x_1,\\ldots,x_n$.\n\n**Module-finite.** $A$ is **module-finite over $R$**, equivalently a **finite\n$R$-algebra**, when $A$ is finitely generated as an $R$-module\n([[def-generated-cyclic-finitely-generated-and-free-modules]]) for the action\n$r\\cdot a=\\eta_A(r)a$.\n\n**Module-finite implies finite type.** If $b_1,\\ldots,b_n$ generate $A$ as an\n$R$-module then $R[b_1,\\ldots,b_n]$, being a subring of $A$ that contains\n$\\eta_A(R)$ and every $b_i$, contains every $R$-linear combination\n$\\sum_i\\eta_A(r_i)b_i$ and hence all of $A$; so $A=R[b_1,\\ldots,b_n]$. The\nconverse fails, and the companion examples page carries a witness.\n\n",
